+# The `btn loading` bug — why the label disappears
+
+## What DaisyUI actually does with `loading`
+
+In current DaisyUI (v3/v4/v5), `loading` is **not** a button modifier. It is a
+**standalone spinner component** meant to be put on its *own* element — a
+`<span>` — e.g. `<span class="loading loading-spinner"></span>`.
+
+The `.loading` class turns whatever element it's on **into the spinner itself**.
+Roughly, its CSS is:
+
+```css
+.loading {
+  pointer-events: none;
+  display: inline-block;
+  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;          /* force a square */
+  width: 1.5rem;                /* fixed spinner size */
+  background-color: currentColor;
+  mask-repeat: no-repeat;
+  mask-position: center;
+  mask-size: 100%;
+  mask-image: url("data:image/svg+xml,<the animated spinner SVG>");
+}
+```
+
+The key mechanism is the **CSS mask**. A mask clips *everything the element
+renders* — background **and text** — down to the shape of the spinner SVG. The
+element is then painted as a solid `currentColor` fill showing through that
+spinner-shaped mask.
+
+## Why the label vanishes and the layout jumps
+
+When `loading` is applied directly to the `<button>` instead of a child span,
+the button element *becomes* the spinner:
+
+1. **Text disappears** — the mask clips the button's entire rendered content to
+   the spinner silhouette. The `"Approving..."` text node is still in the DOM,
+   but it's masked out (and `background-color: currentColor` repaints the whole
+   box), so nothing but the spinner glyph is visible.
+2. **One big spinner filling the button** — the mask is stretched over the full
+   button box (`mask-size: 100%`), so the spinner scales up to the button's size
+   rather than sitting inline at a normal 1–1.5rem.
+3. **Layout jump** — `aspect-ratio: 1 / 1` plus the fixed `width` override the
+   button's normal auto width/padding, so a wide "Approve" pill snaps to a
+   square the instant `isPending` flips. Nothing to do with viewport — it
+   happens identically on desktop and mobile.
+
+So it's not "broken CSS" — it's using a self-contained spinner component as if
+it were the old DaisyUI v2 `btn loading` modifier (which used a `::before`
+pseudo-element and kept the text). That modifier behavior no longer exists.
+
+## The corrected button (idiomatic DaisyUI / SE-2 pattern)
+
+Keep `loading` **off** the button. Render a small inline spinner span **inside**
+the button, next to the still-visible text. `.btn` is already `display: flex`
+with `gap`, so the spinner and label align automatically.
+
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isPending}>
+  {isPending && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-sm" />}
+  {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
+</button>
+```
+
+Why this is right:
+
+- `loading loading-spinner` on a dedicated `<span>` = the spinner lives on its
+  own masked element, so it doesn't clip the label.
+- `loading-sm` keeps it a small inline glyph instead of a full-size spinner.
+- The label stays visible; `.btn`'s flex `gap` spaces the spinner and text (add
+  `mr-2` on the span if you're on a build where the gap isn't applied).
+- `disabled={isPending}` handles interactivity; you no longer need
+  `pointer-events: none` from the `loading` class.
+
+This is the exact Scaffold-ETH 2 convention — a transaction button that shows a
+small spinner beside a visible label while staying disabled until the tx
+confirms onchain.
+
+### Quick lint
+
+Any `"loading"` string in a `btn`'s own `className` is the bug:
+
+```bash
+grep -rn '"loading"' packages/nextjs/app/
+```
+
+- ❌ `className={\`btn btn-primary ${isPending ? "loading" : ""}\`}`
+- ✅ `<span className="loading loading-spinner loading-sm" />` inside the button

